--TEST--
`executeMany` answers the sum of what its runs wrote rather than a `Core\Db\Write`, so a batch offers no `lastId` to read off it — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::executeMany
// `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members` gives the batch a sum and no second return. A `Write`
// answers `lastId` and `changed` off one execution, and a batch has no one
// execution for either to belong to: what `Core\Db\Connection::executeMany`
// reports is what all of its runs changed added together, and the key a single
// `insert` made is only readable where a single `insert` ran.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
array<array<mixed>> $sets = [];
$sets[] = ["ada"];
$sets[] = ["bo"];

// The accepted reading: one whole number for the whole batch.
uint $written = $db->executeMany("insert into accounts (owner) values (?)", $sets);

// And the boundary, which is `execute`'s own answer asked of a batch. A member
// that answered a `Write` here would have to pick one run to describe.
var $batch = $db->executeMany("insert into accounts (owner) values (?)", $sets);
?uint $lastId = $batch->lastId();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0477]: `uint` names no class, so it has no method `lastId`
   --> case.nvs:19:17
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
